\\(n\\),\n  - arch systems having \\(n\\) arches.\n\n[diagram of arches over the sequence 4 2 1 1 2 3 3 4 8 5 5 7 6 6 7 8]\n\nSorting with two stacks\n\nWhat if we have two stacks available in parallel? What’s the story then?\n\nStart  \n3,1,8,6,4,2,7,5  \n1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8  \nFinish\n\nAnswers\n- Sortable permutations do not contain one of an infinite list of patterns (Pratt, 1973).\n- They can be recognised in polynomial time (Even & Itai, 1971).\n- They can be enumerated in polynomial time (A & M Bousquet-Mélou, 2013).\n- They are not in one to one correspondence with:\n  - walks in \\(\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}\\) of semi-length \\(n\\) from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((0,0)\\), or\n  - two-sided arch systems (above and below the line) having \\(n\\) arches, because multiple legal operation sequences can sort the same permutation.\n\nA slight change of perspective\n\n- We can sort \\(\\sigma\\) exactly if we can generate \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) from \\(1,2,\\ldots,n\\).\n- So, given input in order \\(1,2,\\ldots,n\\) what permutations can be generated by arbitrary operation of the two stacks?\n- Can we identify a normal form among operation sequences that generate the same permutation?\n\nProducing 31864275  \n[arch diagram with red arches above and blue arches below the line, labels 3 1 8 6 4 2 7 5]\n\nEager output\n\nIf the next token needed is already in the stacks we might as well output it immediately.\n- In arch systems we disallow the two pictures:  \n  [two small forbidden corner diagrams] and [two small forbidden corner diagrams]\n- In quarter plane walks we forbid a northeast corner formed by a NW or ES factor.  \n  [small NW corner diagram] [small ES corner diagram]\n\nConnected components\n\nFor every cab pattern \\((a<b<c)\\), \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be handled by opposite stacks.\n- Because both must still be in the stacks at the time \\(c\\) is input, and if they were in the same stack they would come out in reversed order.\n- Defining \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) (or their corresponding arches) to be related if this occurs gives us a graph structure. Within a connected component of this graph the choice of stack for any one element forces that for all the others.\n\nNormal form\n\nIf a permutation can be generated by two stacks in parallel, then there is exactly one operation sequence that generates it which outputs eagerly and in which each component begins with a red arch.\n\n[arch diagram with red arches above and blue arches below the line, labels 3 1 8 6 4 2 7 5]\n\nShow me the power series\n\nLet \\(Q(a,u;x,y)\\) be the generating function for walks in \\(\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}\\) starting from \\((0,0)\\): \\(u\\) counts steps, \\(a\\) counts northeast corners, and \\(x,y\\) the coordinates of the endpoint:\n\\[\nQ(a,u;x,y)=1+(x+y)u+(2+2xy+x^2+y^2)u^2\n+((a+4)(x+y)+3x^2y+3xy^2+x^3+y^3)u^3+O(u^4).\n\\]\n\nBy decomposition on the last step or pair of steps we obtain:\n\\[\n(1-u(x+\\bar{x}+y+\\bar{y})-u^2(a-1)(x\\bar{y}+y\\bar{x}))Q=\n1-u\\bar{y}(1+ux(a-1))Q_{y=0}-u\\bar{x}(1+uy(a-1))Q_{x=0}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\bar{x}=1/x,\\ \\bar{y}=1/y\\).\n\nDefining \\(Q(a,u)=Q(a,\\sqrt{u};0,0)\\) we get loops in the quarter plane, and can compute as many coefficients as needed.\n\nMore importantly if \\(S(t)\\) is the generating function for permutations that can be generated by two stacks in parallel then\n\\[\nS(t)=1+t+2t^2+6t^3+23t^4+103t^5+O(t^6)=\\frac{1}{1-S^\\bullet(t)},\n\\]\nwhere \\(S^\\bullet\\) is the unique solution of:\n\\[\nQ\\!\\left(-S^\\bullet;\\frac{t}{(1+S^\\bullet)^2}\\right)=\\frac{1+S^\\bullet}{1-S^\\bullet}.\n\\]\n\nThis arises from manipulations of the functional equations that connect \\(Q\\) with \\(S\\) through the normal form described above.\n\nCan we find asymptotic information about the coefficients, \\(s_n\\), of \\(S\\)?\n\nAnd the algebraic analytic combinatorics\n\nTo find the radius of convergence of \\(S^\\bullet\\) we must explore singularities of \\(Q(a,u)\\) when \\(a<0\\).  \nConjecture 1† The generating function of quarter plane loops is \\((a+1)\\)-positive:\n\\[\nQ(a,u)=\\sum_{n\\ge 0} p_n(a+1)u^n\n\\]\nwhere \\(p_n(b)\\in \\mathbb{N}[b]\\).\n\nLet \\(\\rho_Q(a)\\) denote the radius of convergence (in \\(u\\)) of \\(Q(a,u)\\), and \\(t_c\\) the radius of convergence of \\(S^\\bullet(t)\\). We can plot \\(\\rho_Q(a)\\) and \\(( -S^\\bullet(t),\\, t(1+S^\\bullet(t))^{-2})\\) in the \\((a,u)\\) plane for \\(t\\in[0,t_c]\\). They must intersect at \\(t=t_c\\).\n\n[plot with axes \\(a\\) and \\(u\\), curves labeled \\(\\rho_Q(a)\\), \\(t=t_c\\), and \\(( -S^\\bullet(t), t(1+S^\\bullet(t))^{-2})\\)]\n\nConjecture 2† The radius of convergence of \\(Q(a,\\cdot)\\) is\n\\[\n\\rho_Q(a)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\frac{1}{(2+\\sqrt{2+2a})^2} & \\text{if } a\\ge -1/2,\\\\[4pt]\n-\\frac{a}{2(1-a)^2} & \\text{if } -1\\le a\\le -1/2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFPSAC 2014, Chicago, USA (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/2425/pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.4 years ago; Permutations sortable by two stacks in ... Subsequently, Pratt and Tarjan asked about permutations that can be sorted using two stacks in parallel. ... considered by Knuth who dealt with the case where the device was a stack, i.e. a storage mechanism ... 1365–8050 c⃝2014 Discrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science (DMTCS), Nancy, France\nFPSAC 2014, Chicago, USA\nDMTCS proc. AT, 2014, 585–596\nPermutations sortable by two stacks in\nparallel and quarter plane walks\nMichael Albert1∗and Mireille Bousquet-Mélou2†\n1Department of Computer Science, University of Otago, New Zealand\n2CNRS, LaBRI, Université de Bordeaux, France\nAbstract. At the end of the 1960s, Knuth characterised in terms of forbidden patterns the permutations that can be\nsorted using a stack. He also showed that they are in bijection with Dyck paths and thus counted by the Catalan\nnumbers. Subsequently, Pratt and Tarjan asked about permutations that can be sorted using two stacks in parallel.\nThis question is signiﬁcantly harder, and the associated counting question has remained open for 40 years. We solve\nit by giving a pair of equations that characterise the generating function of such permutations.\nThe ﬁrst component of this system describes the generating function Q(a, u) of square lattice loops conﬁned to the\npositive quadrant, counted by the length and the number of North-West and East-South factors. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by two stacks in parallel and quarter plane walks - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S019566981400136X)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel and quarter plane walks\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2014.08.024 Get rights and content ... In particular, a sortable permutation can now be sorted by several distinct sequences of stack operations. ... We solve it by giving a pair of functional equations that characterise the generating function of permutations that can be sorted with two parallel stacks.\n\n## European Journal of Combinatorics\n\nVolume 43, January 2015, Pages 131-164\n\n# Permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel and quarter plane walks\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2014.08.024Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nAt the end of the 1960s, Knuth characterised the permutations that can be sorted using a stack in terms of forbidden patterns. He also showed that they are in bijection with Dyck paths and thus counted by the Catalan numbers. Subsequently, Even & Itai, Pratt and Tarjan studied permutations that can be sorted using two stacks in parallel. This problem is significantly harder. In particular, a sortable permutation can now be sorted by several distinct sequences of stack operations. Moreover, in order to be sortable, a permutation must avoid infinitely many patterns. The associated counting question has remained open for 40 years. We solve it by giving a pair of functional equations that characterise the generating function of permutations that can be sorted with two parallel stacks.\n\nThe first component of this system describes the generating function of square lattice loops confined to the positive quadrant, counted by the length and the number of North-West and East-South factors. Our analysis of the asymptotic number of sortable permutations relies at the moment on two intriguing conjectures dealing with the series . We prove that they hold for loops confined to the upper half plane, or not confined at all. They remain open for quarter plane loops. Given the recent activity on walks confined to cones, we believe them to be attractive per se.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by two stacks in parallel  and quarter plane walks (https://www.labri.fr/perso/bousquet/Exposes/versailles.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.8 years ago; Conjecture 1: $(a + 1)$-Positivity ... The generating function of quarter plane loops is $(a + 1)$-positive: ... with $p_n(b)\\in \\mathbb{N}[b]$.\nConjecture 1: $(a + 1)$-Positivity\n\nConjecture\n\nThe generating function of quarter plane loops is $(a + 1)$-positive:\n\n\\[\nQ(a,u)=\\sum_{n\\ge 0} u^n p_n(a+1)\n\\]\n\nwith $p_n(b)\\in \\mathbb{N}[b]$.\n\nRefinement\n\n- One can count separately vertical and horizontal steps:\n\n\\[\nQ(a,s,t)=\\sum_{i,j\\ge 0} s^i t^j p_{i,j}(a+1)\n\\]\n\n- True up to $n=200$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel (https://pp2017.github.io/assets/pdf/abstracts/talks/andrew_elvey_price.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Permutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel ... single stack are exactly those which avoid the pattern 312. ... A deque-sortable or tsip-sortable permutation can be classiﬁed by its operation sequence,\nPermutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel\nAndrew Elvey Price\nandrewelveyprice@gmail.com\nUniversity of Melbourne, Australia\nThis talk is based on joint work with Tony Guttmann.\nIn his seminal work The Art of Computer Programming [2], Knuth was the ﬁrst to consider\na number of classic data structures from the point of view of the permutations they could\nproduce from the identity permutation, or equinumerously, the permutations which the\ndata structure can sort. Famously, he noticed that the permutations obtainable using a\nsingle stack are exactly those which avoid the pattern 312. Knuth posed the same question\nfor a number of other data structures, in particular, he asked how many permutations of\neach length can be obtained using a double ended queue (deque) or two stacks in parallel\n(tsip).\nIn [1], Albert and Bousquet-Melou characterised the counting function P(t) for tsip-\nsortable permutations in terms of a generating function Q(u, a) for weighted quarter plane\nloops. This solves the enumeration problem for tsip-sortable permutations in the sense\nthat P is characterised by a system of functional equations. Using a similar method to\nAlbert and Bousquet-Melou, we present a solution to the problem of enumerating deque-\nsortable permutations. Our solution takes the form of a simple relationship between the\ncounting function D(t) for deque-sortable permutations and the counting function P(t).\nThe allowed operations for two stacks in parallel (left) and a deque (right)\nA deque-sortable or tsip-sortable permutation can be classiﬁed by its operation sequence,\nthat is, a sequence of moves I1, I2, O1, O2 which sorts the permutation. We will call an\noperation sequence for a double ended queue a deque-word, and we call an operation\nsequence for two stacks in parallel a tsip-word. Since many operation sequences correspond\nto the same permutation, we proceed by deﬁning canonical deque-words, which are in\nbijection with the permutations they sort.\nA deque-word is called canonical if it has the following properties:\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2016.08.002)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Recently Albert and Bousquet-Mélou (2015) obtained the solution to the long-standing problem of the number of permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel (tsip). ... This is a consequence of the fact that only 231-avoiding permutations are stack-sortable, as we discuss below. ... As remarked above, simple stacks can sort any permutation that does not contain three successive (but not necessarily consecutive) elements in the order 231. ... The series start: 𝑃⁡(𝑡)=1+𝑡+2⁢𝑡2+6⁢𝑡3+23⁢𝑡4+103⁢𝑡5+513⁢𝑡6+2760⁢𝑡7+15741⁢𝑡8+⋯tsips,𝐷⁡(𝑡)=1+𝑡+2⁢𝑡2+6⁢𝑡3+24⁢𝑡4+116⁢𝑡5+634⁢𝑡6+3762⁢𝑡7+23638⁢𝑡8+⋯deques.\n\n## European Journal of Combinatorics\n\nVolume 59, January 2017, Pages 71-95\n\n# Permutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2016.08.002Get rights and content\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nOpen archive\n\n## Abstract\n\nRecently Albert and Bousquet-Mélou (2015) obtained the solution to the long-standing problem of the number of permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel (tsip). Their solution was expressed in terms of functional equations. We show that the equally long-standing problem of the number of permutations sortable by a double-ended queue (deque) can be simply related to the solution of the same functional equations. Subject to plausible, but unproved, conditions, the radius of convergence of both generating functions is the same. Numerical work confirms this conjecture to 15 significant digits. Further numerical work suggests that the coefficients of the deque generating function behave as 𝜅𝑑 ⋅𝜇𝑛 ⋅𝑛−3/2, where 𝜇 =8.28140220763657…, while the coefficients of the corresponding tsip generating function behave as 𝜅𝑝 ⋅𝜇𝑛 ⋅𝑛𝛾 with 𝛾 ≈−2.47326. This is a consequence of the fact that only 231-avoiding permutations are stack-sortable, as we discuss below.\n\nShortly thereafter, a number of authors, notably Even and Itai  [7], Pratt  [11] and Tarjan  [12] considered more general data structures. Foremost among these were two stacks in parallel, two stacks in series and deques. A deque, illustrated in Fig. 2, is a double-ended queue, with insertions and deletions allowed at either end. Until recently  [2], none of these had been solved. As remarked above, simple stacks can sort any permutation that does not contain three successive (but not necessarily consecutive) elements in the order 231. We write this as the class 𝐴𝑣⁡(231), that is, the class of 231-avoiding permutations. For example 1 573 642 cannot be sorted by a stack as the elements 562 (among other sub-sequences) are in the forbidden relative order. Input-restricted deques are describable by the class 𝐴𝑣⁡(4231,3241), whereas Pratt  [11] showed that the analogous description for deques (without input or output restrictions) requires infinitely many minimal forbidden patterns.\n\nTo establish a notation, let 𝑝𝑛 denote the number of permutations of length 𝑛 that can be produced by two parallel stacks, let 𝑑𝑛 be the corresponding quantity for deques, and let 𝑠𝑛 be the corresponding quantity for two stacks in series. We name the corresponding generating functions 𝑃⁡(𝑡)=∑𝑝𝑛⁢𝑡𝑛,𝐷⁡(𝑡)=∑𝑑𝑛⁢𝑡𝑛,and𝑆⁡(𝑡)=∑𝑠𝑛⁢𝑡𝑛.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by deques and two stacks in parallel share the same growth rate (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.00056)\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Recently Albert and Bousquet-M\\'elou obtained the solution to the long-standing problem of the enumeration of permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel (2sip).\nTitle: Permutations sortable by deques and two stacks in parallel share the same growth rate\nAuthors: Andrew Elvey Price\nDate: Fri Nov 29 20:01:06 2019\n\nRecently Albert and Bousquet-M\\'elou obtained the solution to the long-standing problem of the enumeration of permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel (2sip). Their solution was expressed in terms of functional equations. E.P. and Guttmann then showed that the equally long-standing problem of the number of permutations sortable by a double ended queue (deque) can be simply related to the solution of the same functional equations. They then conjectured that the radius of convergence of both generating functions is the same, and reduced this conjecture to a series of conjectures of Albert and Bousquet-M\\'elou regarding a generating function for quarter-plane loops. In this note we prove that the two growth rates are equal, using a combinatorial argument on certain lattice paths which are in bijection with the two classes. As a corollary we prove that the generating function P(t) for permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel satisfies an inequality which was conjectured by Albert and Bousquet-M\\'elou.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by two stacks in parallel and quarter plane walks (https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.4487)\nciteturn1academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Title: Permutations sortable by two stacks in parallel and quarter plane walks ... In particular, a sortable permutation can now be sorted by several distinct sequences of stack operations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPermutations sortable by deques and by two stacks in parallel (https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02273)\nciteturn1academia20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Title: Permut